The nostalgia factory that=E2=80=99s made millions flipping old Polaroids

In Wisconsin, a team of millennials is working to sell you your childhood

BY KATHERINE LAIDLAW (https://x.com/klaidlaw?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--pQ_PzFshY1w4=
DOTGbziGu0vxz3WDf6JRqsnUDtJc7RBJZccyYASqKz-n4JeLewDcHKy5kUxEbhrB1J61ejDsQEi=
Wj8Q&_hsmi=3D400085604 )

As a child, Kori Fuerst was brimming with business ideas. She recorded radi=
o shows on cassette tapes, and charged her neighbors admission prices to li=
sten to them. She ground up leaves in the woods near her house, bottled up =
the juice, called it insect repellent, and put it up for sale.

=E2=80=9CReally, just any way we could shake down our parents and neighbors=
 for money,=E2=80=9D she says, laughing.

These days, she=E2=80=99s still making money off cassette tapes, though now=
 she owns the business and the revenue is in the millions.

Like many millennials, Fuerst is riding a wave of nostalgia. She yearns for=
 the days of Pok=C3=A9mon cards and spends weekends searching for Pixar=E2=
=80=99s =E2=80=9CCars=E2=80=9D on VHS =E2=80=94 a rarity in the vintage col=
lectibles world. And, alongside her husband Adam, she=E2=80=99s built Retro=
spekt (http://retrospekt.com/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--pQ_PzFshY1w4DOTGbziGu0vxz3W=
Df6JRqsnUDtJc7RBJZccyYASqKz-n4JeLewDcHKy5kUxEbhrB1J61ejDsQEiWj8Q&_hsmi=3D40=
0085604 ) , a retro-tech empire in Milwaukee, turning a fascination for yes=
teryear and Polaroid cameras into a career.

The Apple of its time

When Kori and Adam Fuerst first met in 2007, they were both big fans of Pol=
aroid.

Once called =E2=80=9Cthe Apple of its time,=E2=80=9D the company controlled=
 nearly two-thirds of the instant-camera market in the US at its peak in th=
e 1970s.

Digital cameras forced the company, which relied on film sales, into bankru=
ptcy for the first time in 2001.

Artist Andy Warhol snaps author and model Tessa Dahl at a party in 1973. (P=
hoto by Tim Boxer/Hulton Archive/Getty Images)

When the company declared bankruptcy a second time, right around when the F=
uersts started dating, Polaroid stopped making film, and the price of 10-sh=
ot packs shot up to $30-$40 on eBay. The Fuersts thought the cameras, which=
 were selling for a few bucks at antique shops, were likely to be donated w=
ith unused film sitting inside, so many of their early dates were spent tra=
wling Goodwill.

That left them with a surplus of ~100 Polaroids, minus the film, and when a=
 company called the Impossible Project resurrected the brand, post-bankrupt=
cy, the Fuersts became pickers for the company. They=E2=80=99d sell their f=
indings to the Netherlands-based company, which would then restore the old =
cameras to like-new condition.

=E2=80=9CWhen I think back to the early days, I remember feeling almost eup=
horic that this hobby that we had was suddenly profitable,=E2=80=9D Kori sa=
ys. =E2=80=9CThat felt amazing.=E2=80=9D

Kori and Adam Fuerst have collaborated with Sanrio, Mattel and Pepsi, among=
 others, issuing Polaroids with a range of familiar colors and characters. =
(Courtesy of Retrospekt)

The next year, the Fuersts began to manage a team of pickers across the cou=
ntry, still supplying stock to the Impossible Project.

Alongside their thrifting pursuits, they were in grad school working toward=
 degrees in occupational therapy (Adam) and speech pathology (Kori). Two ye=
ars into his career at the hospital, Adam realized that he=E2=80=99d make m=
ore money if he stayed home and worked on Retrospekt.

=E2=80=9CWhen we started it, it was like, =E2=80=98can we pay our utilities=
 bill? Can we pay for some groceries? Can we pay for books?=E2=80=99=E2=80=
=9D Adam says. =E2=80=9CWe just moved the goalpost a little bit, and at one=
 point I was like, =E2=80=98Oh, can we buy a car?=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D

The Milwaukee workshop where 500k items have been refurbished. (Courtesy of=
 Retrospekt)

In 2014, the Impossible Project asked if they could learn how to refurbish =
the cameras themselves. They had no technical or business expertise. They s=
aid yes.

The Impossible Project sent over a trainer from the Netherlands who=E2=80=
=99d repaired cameras for Polaroid for more than 30 years. He didn=E2=80=99=
t speak English, but he did speak Polaroid. He left behind a set of tools t=
hat Retrospekt replicated.

They=E2=80=99ve since refurbished 300k cameras.

Beyond Polaroid

Retrospekt, with headquarters in an airy warehouse in Milwaukee, hired its =
first staffers using posters and pull-tabs. Today, they employ ~40 people, =
who clean and repair every one of the 500k+ items that has come through the=
ir doors.

In recent years, they=E2=80=99ve expanded into other kinds of retro tech: c=
assette tapes, CDs, Tamagotchis. They also manufacture their own Polaroid c=
ameras, wholesale, and have collaborated with Sanrio on Hello Kitty cameras=
 and Mattel on Barbie editions. They sell on their website, or to major ret=
ailers like Urban Outfitters and Nordstrom.

A technician restores an old Polaroid in Retrospekt=E2=80=99s workshop. (Co=
urtesy of Retrospekt)

We=E2=80=99ve been in an era of the reboot for what feels like years. Every=
where, companies are making what=E2=80=99s old cool again. Hollywood=E2=80=
=99s top-grossing films last year, like most years, were all sequels or rem=
akes. Motorola produced a line of flip phones last year, calling back to th=
eir popular =E2=80=9800s Razrs. In 2023, Hasbro even launched a reissued Fu=
rby doll.

Retro tech has been on the rise for years, too. In 2010, 2.8m vinyl records=
 were sold in the US By 2024, that number reached 43.6m.

Still, it=E2=80=99s a huge leap to go from spending $100 on a Smiths casset=
te tape (https://retrospekt.com/products/the-smiths-the-queen-is-dead-casse=
tte-tape?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--pQ_PzFshY1w4DOTGbziGu0vxz3WDf6JRqsnUDtJc7RBJZccy=
YASqKz-n4JeLewDcHKy5kUxEbhrB1J61ejDsQEiWj8Q&_hsmi=3D400085604 ) that remind=
s you of riding shotgun on trips to the hardware store with your dad to $2m=
 on a sealed Super Mario Bros. video game. And yet. (https://www.nytimes.co=
m/2021/08/06/business/super-mario-bros-sale-record.html?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--p=
Q_PzFshY1w4DOTGbziGu0vxz3WDf6JRqsnUDtJc7RBJZccyYASqKz-n4JeLewDcHKy5kUxEbhrB=
1J61ejDsQEiWj8Q&_hsmi=3D400085604 )

In 2021, an anonymous buyer spent $2m on a still-sealed 1985 Super Mario Br=
os. game, breaking the previous record of $1.56m for the highest price paid=
 for a video game.

The transaction happened on a platform called Rally, a stock market for col=
lectibles. Rally (http://rallyrd.com/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--pQ_PzFshY1w4DOTGbzi=
Gu0vxz3WDf6JRqsnUDtJc7RBJZccyYASqKz-n4JeLewDcHKy5kUxEbhrB1J61ejDsQEiWj8Q&_h=
smi=3D400085604 ) , which started ~10 years ago, was created on the belief =
that even if investors couldn=E2=80=99t afford to buy a museum-quality car =
or watch, they=E2=80=99d spend money to buy a part of one.

Olivia Heller/The Hustle

Essentially, Rally runs tiny IPOs on collectibles, allowing the market to d=
ecide how much they=E2=80=99re worth after investors have bought a portion =
of the assets. Imagine a real estate portfolio, but for classic cars, watch=
es, baseball cards and, of course, video games.

The company had spent $140k to acquire the video game just a year and a hal=
f before.

When Rob Petrozzo and his co-founders started Rally, their first purchase w=
as a 1977 Lotus Esprit. =E2=80=9CAny boy growing up, you think of the poste=
r on the wall of the Lamborghini or Ferrari. It becomes a part of our perso=
nality,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9CTo us, the Esprit was an untapped gem. T=
hat car is what Elon based Tesla on. It just looks and feels very futuristi=
c for its time.=E2=80=9D

They brought the car to the platform in a $46k IPO. It =E2=80=9Cexited=E2=
=80=9D to its new owner for $80k.

=E2=80=9CWhen we bring something new to market now, we want to make sure it=
=E2=80=99s extremely relevant but tells a story our users have never heard =
before,=E2=80=9D he says.

A quick perusal of Rally=E2=80=99s current offerings includes:

- A Pokemon first edition booster box (suggested market cap $23k)
- An =E2=80=9818 Shohei Ohtani rookie card (suggested market cap $78.7k)
- A =E2=80=9894 Lamborghini Diablo (sold for $975k)
- An =E2=80=9889 Nintendo Game Boy (suggested market cap $5.6k)

The high of nostalgia

Nostalgia, it turns out, is a hell of a drug. Research shows it can make us=
 more inclined (https://thedecisionlab.com/biases/nostalgia-effect?_hsenc=
=3Dp2ANqtz--pQ_PzFshY1w4DOTGbziGu0vxz3WDf6JRqsnUDtJc7RBJZccyYASqKz-n4JeLewD=
cHKy5kUxEbhrB1J61ejDsQEiWj8Q&_hsmi=3D400085604 ) to spend money, and more l=
ikely (https://www.ivey.uwo.ca/impact/read/2025/12/ask-the-experts-the-powe=
r-of-nostalgia-in-marketing/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--pQ_PzFshY1w4DOTGbziGu0vxz3WD=
f6JRqsnUDtJc7RBJZccyYASqKz-n4JeLewDcHKy5kUxEbhrB1J61ejDsQEiWj8Q&_hsmi=3D400=
085604 ) to donate to charity.

Beyond that, it has a whole host of other social benefits, according to Eva=
n Weingarten, assistant professor of marketing at the University of Souther=
n California=E2=80=99s Marshall School of Business.

=E2=80=9CFor many hundreds of years, nostalgia was seen as bad. You=E2=80=
=99re stuck in the past,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9COver the last 20 years,=
 researchers have said, nostalgia=E2=80=99s actually pretty good for you.=
=E2=80=9D

People who feel more nostalgic see their social connectedness go up =E2=80=
=94 they feel more loved, have a stronger sense of self, and are more optim=
istic and hopeful. Nostalgic advertisements tend to perform better.

And there=E2=80=99s something else at play here. In an increasingly uncerta=
in world, as the cost of living goes up and up, we use nostalgia to anchor =
us to simpler times.

Olivia Heller/The Hustle

Weingarten himself isn=E2=80=99t immune to nostalgia=E2=80=99s charms. When=
 he browsed Retrospekt=E2=80=99s website, he says, he hovered over a Ninten=
do 64.

=E2=80=9CFor a brief moment, I was like, is this too much to spend?=E2=80=
=9D he says. =E2=80=9CI had to stop myself.=E2=80=9D

In Retrospekt

From =E2=80=9CStranger Things=E2=80=9D to Y2K fashion, Kori and Adam Fuerst=
 have had a front-row seat to pop culture=E2=80=99s continued glorification=
 of all things bygone. For Kori, 2025 was the year of the cassette, and the=
 couple has watched sales of Kate Bush=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9CHounds of Love=E2=
=80=9D tape spike thanks to =E2=80=9CStranger Things=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D new =
(and final) season.

And while they=E2=80=99ve had interest from outside investors, Adam Fuerst =
has no interest in watching private equity or venture capital dilute what t=
hey=E2=80=99ve managed to deliver to customers across the globe so far.

=E2=80=9CI think our goals are different than traditional investment,=E2=80=
=9D he says.

=E2=80=9CWe want to create moments of magic for our customers and have fun =
with what we do and enjoy going to work and be able to pivot as we need to =
to things that are interesting and compelling and relevant to what we do an=
d relevant to what=E2=80=99s happening in the world.=E2=80=9D

First launched in 2006, 20 years later you can still buy an iPod shuffle fr=
om Retrospekt for ~$100. (Photo by Sebastien Meyer/Getty Images)

In the age of subscriptions, owning physical things is new again. Flipping =
a record isn=E2=80=99t inconvenient; it=E2=80=99s an experience.

=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s hard to know anytime you=E2=80=99re on the internet a=
nymore if you=E2=80=99re experiencing reality or if something=E2=80=99s bee=
n altered,=E2=80=9D Adam says.

=E2=80=9CAnd I think it=E2=80=99s just a quick and easy way to go and touch=
 grass.=E2=80=9D

