<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">In 2014, the Impossible Project asked if they=
 could learn how to refurbish the cameras themselves. They had no technical=
 or business expertise. They said yes.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">The Impossible Project sent over a trainer fr=
om the Netherlands who=E2=80=99d repaired cameras for Polaroid for more tha=
n 30 years. He didn=E2=80=99t speak English, but he did speak Polaroid. He =
left behind a set of tools that Retrospekt replicated.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">They=E2=80=99ve since refurbished <strong>300=
k</strong> cameras.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<h2 style=3D"margin:0; line-height:125%; font-size:18px">Beyond Polaroid</h=
2>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">Retrospekt, with headquarters in an airy ware=
house in Milwaukee, hired its first staffers using posters and pull-tabs. T=
oday, they employ ~40 people, who clean and repair every one of the <strong=
>500k+</strong> items that has come through their doors.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:125%">In recent years, they=E2=80=99ve expanded int=
o other kinds of retro tech: cassette tapes, CDs, Tamagotchis. They also ma=
nufacture their own Polaroid cameras, wholesale, and have collaborated with=
 Sanrio on Hello Kitty cameras and Mattel on Barbie editions. They sell on =
their website, or to major retailers like Urban Outfitters and Nordstrom.&n=
bsp;</p></div></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
